The '''Kingdom of Ufyen''' ([[Burawi language|Burawi]]: ''Boqortooyadii Ufyen'') was a highland monarchy in southern [[Southwestern Kaftia]], centred on the [[Lake Haro]] basin and the [[Kushish]] highlands. Founded around 1,000 BCE with the development of iron smelting, Ufyen was the peninsula's first state-level polity and the dominant power of the southern highlands for nearly two millennia. The kingdom's history is conventionally divided into four periods — the [[Geedi Period|Geedi]], [[Dalmar Period|Dalmar]], [[Warfaa Period|Warfaa]], and [[Saleebaan Period|Saleebaan]] — encompassing nine attested dynasties and one interregnum. It was conquered and absorbed by the [[Kingdom of Burawa]] during the [[Northern Conquest of Burawa]] (893–925 AD).

The kingdom's power derived from its control of the [[Kushish]] iron mines, the strategic mountain passes of the [[Great Kaftian Mountain Range]], and the upper reaches of the [[Buran River]], the peninsula's principal trade route. Its political system combined military authority with religious legitimacy: the king served as chief priest of [[Waaq]], and coronation rites were performed at the summit shrine of [[Buur Waaq]].

== Etymology ==

The kingdom takes its name from the city of '''[[Ufyen (city)|Ufyen]]''', located north of [[Lake Haro]], which served as the ceremonial and religious capital where Ufyen kings were crowned. The political capital was [[Badalga-Jir]] and the commercial centre was [[Jowhar]], but the kingdom retained the name of its sacred city throughout its history.

== Geography ==

The Kingdom of Ufyen occupied the elevated terrain of the [[Lake Haro]] basin and the surrounding [[Kushish]] highlands, in the south-central portion of [[Southwestern Kaftia]]. The kingdom's core territory comprised three key areas:

* '''[[Lake Haro]]''': An 8,850 km² lake system, mostly in modern [[Burawa]], on whose shores the capital [[Badalga-Jir]] was situated on a defensible peninsula.
* '''[[Kushish]] highlands''': The mineral-rich mountain zone containing the iron and copper deposits that formed the basis of the kingdom's economy.
* '''Upper [[Buran River]] valley''': The river corridor connecting the highlands to the [[Mesogean Sea]] coast, controlled through the customs post at [[Jowhar]].

At its greatest extent under the late [[Geedi Period]] (~700 BCE), the kingdom also controlled the [[Galbeed River]] valley and the coastal lowlands as far as the [[Mesogean Sea]]. The [[Dafar River|Dafar]] and [[Hooyal River|Hooyal]] valleys to the west were never incorporated. To the east, the [[Bariites|Bariite]] peoples of the [[Orma]] highlands remained outside sustained Ufyen control and were a persistent source of military pressure.

== Dynasties ==

The history of the Kingdom of Ufyen is conventionally divided into four periods, each named after the dominant or founding dynasty. The [[Geedi Period|Geedi Period]], predating the adoption of alphabetic writing, is documented primarily through [[Farta Geedi]] logographic inscriptions and later Dalmar-era retrospective accounts. The subsequent periods are attested through [[Mesogean script|Echian script]] records and are divided into nine individually named dynasties.

